{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChDCQ7Rz6DntYDVSYaeMn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found no vulnerability in `copy_fd_bitmaps` and wrote that verdict to `results/s065.json`: `is_vulnerable: false`, `cwe: null`, no vulnerable statements.\n\n**Why it's safe:**\n- **`open_fds` and `close_on_exec`:** each copies exactly `copy_words * BITS_PER_LONG` bits from the old table. Everything after that, up to the new table's size (`nwords * BITS_PER_LONG`), is set to zero.\n- **`full_fds_bits`:** this bitmap has one bit per word of `open_fds`, so copying `copy_words` bits and extending to `nwords` bits is the correct size.\n- **No stale bits:** because it uses `bitmap_copy_and_extend`, nothing beyond the copied range carries over into the new table. This appears to be the patched form of the function. Older kernels did this with `memcpy`/`memset` sized in bytes. I recall a bug where `close_range()` with `CLOSE_RANGE_UNSHARE` could leave these bitmaps inconsistent, but that's from memory, not from this file.\n\nThe one thing this file can't confirm is that the caller passes a `copy_words` no larger than either table's word count. I noted that limit in the `suggested_fix` field."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":980,"cache_read_input_tokens":22158,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":980},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e0f6232-b5c8-4cd4-b325-e41c3c335ba0","uuid":"d8aea641-48c2-46d4-a403-70ef14005946","timestamp":"2026-11-20T00:01:01.614Z","request_id":"req_011ChDCQ7FZoWfjyMaNvpXxA"}
